1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,300
Our brain is probably the most important organ in our body.
我们的大脑可能是我们身体中最重要的器官。

2
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:12,010
It's an incredibly complex thing that helps us think, speak, feel and move.
它极其复杂，帮助我们思考、说话、感知和行动。

3
00:00:12,010 --> 00:00:20,800
It's no wonder we need to look after it and just like the rest of our body, we need to keep it exercised to keep it healthy.
难怪我们需要照顾它，而且就像我们身体的其他部分一样，我们需要让它得到锻炼以保持健康。

4
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:30,610
Our brain is made up of 86 billion neurons - or nerve cells - but as we get older some of these slowly die off.
我们的大脑由860亿个神经元（即神经细胞）组成，但随着我们年龄的增长，其中一些会慢慢死亡。

5
00:00:30,610 --> 00:00:44,150
In extreme cases, nerve cells in different areas of the brain become damaged and eventually die causing a condition called dementia where a person is unable to perform certain mental functions.
在极端情况下，大脑不同区域的神经细胞会受到损伤，最终死亡，从而导致一种叫做痴呆症的疾病，患者无法执行某些心智功能。

6
00:00:44,150 --> 00:00:50,650
It's a good idea to train our brain by learning ways to increase our memory or intelligence.
通过学习提高记忆力或智力的方法来训练我们的大脑是个好主意。

7
00:00:50,650 --> 00:00:55,580
I like to stretch my brain by doing a crossword or a Sudoku puzzle.
我喜欢通过做填字游戏或九宫格谜题来锻炼我的大脑。

8
00:00:55,580 --> 00:01:01,490
There's also evidence that learning a second language can boost your brainpower.
也有证据表明，学习第二语言可以增强你的脑力。

9
00:01:01,490 --> 00:01:16,980
Dr Catherine Loveday, who's a neuropsychologist, thinks being bilingual is a protective factor for the brain because it "involves a lot of switchings and that seems to exercise the sort of executive parts of our brain.
神经心理学家凯瑟琳·洛夫迪博士认为，成为双语者可以保护大脑，因为“这涉及到大量的转换，这似乎能锻炼我们大脑的执行区域。

10
00:01:16,980 --> 00:01:35,070
Those parts of the brain are kind of stronger and fitter when it comes to resisting some kind of damage from a stroke."
当涉及到抵御中风所带来的某种损害时，大脑的这些部分会更强壮、更健康。”

11
00:01:35,070 --> 00:01:49,770
The main aim of trying these skills is to stimulate the brain.
尝试这些技能的主要目的是刺激大脑。

12
00:01:49,770 --> 00:02:02,330
This is something Dr. Loveday describes as building up our cognitive reserve - that's building up extra abilities to help protect the brain against declining memory or thinking.
洛夫迪博士将此描述为建立我们的认知储备——也就是培养额外的能力，以帮助保护大脑免受记忆力或思维能力下降的影响。

13
00:02:02,330 --> 00:02:10,920
She says "even if people take up languages or take up other things later in life it will give them a degree of protection."
她说：“即使人们在晚年才开始学习语言或其他事情，这也会给他们一定程度的保护。”

14
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:14,860
As I get older, that has put my mind at rest!
随着我年龄的增长，这让我安心了不少！

15
00:02:14,860 --> 00:02:25,400
The best bit of research I have read is that eating chocolate may enhance our ability to acquire and utilise knowledge - that's our cognitive performance.
我读过的最棒的研究是，吃巧克力可能会提高我们获取和运用知识的能力——也就是我们的认知表现。

16
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:31,980
It may not be healthy for the rest of my body but at least it might make me a brain box!
这对我身体的其他部分可能不健康，但至少它可能会让我成为一个聪明人！

17
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:33,400
What do you think?
你怎么看呢？
